{"parentUuid":"02cf2e93-5922-4875-8ece-e06d16116587","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"cfe22842-ba08-4ec5-a7d1-14fbd50c5f8f","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CLngZuWRmYsn9CgbLcSo8W","type":"tool_result","content":"110\t## Pre-Push Publication\n111\t\n112\tThe pre-push hook reads pending publications before pushing active v2 refs.\n113\t\n114\tFor pending local rotations, pre-push:\n115\t\n116\t1. Drops stale pending reset publications until the newest queued\n117\t   `reset_full_current_root_hash` is in the live local `/full/current` history.\n118\t   This covers the crash or failure window where a marker was recorded but the\n119\t   reset never happened.\n120\t2. Pushes the remaining pending archived generation refs together.\n121\t3. Reads the current remote `/full/current` hash.\n122\t4. Pushes local `/full/current` using `--force-with-lease`, guarded by the\n123\t   expected old remote current hash.\n124\t5. Removes the pending marker only after archive refs and the current reset are\n125\t   published successfully.\n126\t\n127\tThe guarded current update is the key difference from a normal push. Replacing\n128\tremote `/full/current` with a fresh orphan generation is not a fast-forward, so\n129\tnormal `git push` rejects it. The lease makes the reset explicit and safe: it\n130\tonly succeeds if the remote current is still the old generation covered by the\n131\tlocal archive.\n132\t\n133\t### Example: Local Rotation With No New Work\n134\t\n135\t```text\n136\tBefore rotation:\n137\t  remote /full/current = C100\n138\t  local  /full/current = C100\n139\t\n140\tLocal rotation:\n141\t  local /full/0000000000007 = A7, parented on C100\n142\t  local /full/current = N7, a fresh orphan root\n143\t  pending marker expects remote current C100\n144\t\n145\tPre-push:\n146\t  push /full/0000000000007\n147\t  force-with-lease /full/current from C100 to N7\n148\t  clear pending marker\n149\t```\n150\t\n151\tFinal remote state:\n152\t\n153\t```text\n154\t/full/0000000000007 contains the old generation\n155\t/full/current is empty and contains none of the archived checkpoint IDs\n156\t```\n157\t\n158\t### Example: Local Rotation, Then More Work Before Push\n159\t\n160\t```text\n161\tLocal rotation:\n162\t  /full/0000000000007 contains old checkpoint IDs 1..100\n163\t  /full/current starts at orphan root N7\n164\t\n165\tMore local checkpoints are written:\n166\t  /full/current advances from N7 to C105 and contains IDs 101..105\n167\t\n168\tPre-push:\n169\t  push /full/0000000000007\n170\t  force-with-lease remote /full/current from old current to C105\n171\t```\n172\t\n173\tThe remote archived generation still contains only IDs 1..100. Remote\n174\t`/full/current` contains only IDs 101..105. The current generation does not need\n175\tto be empty at push time; it only needs to descend from the recorded\n176\t`reset_full_current_root_hash`.\n177\t\n178\t### Example: Multiple Local Rotations Before One Push\n179\t\n180\t```text\n181\tLocal state:\n182\t  /full/0000000000007 contains IDs 1..100\n183\t  /full/0000000000008 contains IDs 101..200\n184\t  /full/current contains IDs 201..205\n185\t\n186\tPending publications:\n187\t  publication for /full/0000000000007\n188\t  publication for /full/0000000000008\n189\t```\n190\t\n191\tPre-push pushes both archived refs, then resets remote `/full/current` to the\n192\tlatest local current head. Cleanup can later delete generations 7 and 8\n193\tindependently.\n194\t\n195\t## Normal Non-Fast-Forward Recovery\n196\t\n197\tFor ordinary additive divergence on `/main` or `/full/current`, recovery uses a\n198\ttree merge:\n199\t\n200\t1. Fetch the remote ref to a temporary local ref.\n201\t2. Flatten the local and remote trees.\n202\t3. Combine entries by path.\n203\t4. Build a merged tree and commit.\n204\t5. Retry the push.\n205\t\n206\tThis works because checkpoint data is sharded by checkpoint ID. Different\n207\tcheckpoints normally land at different paths.\n208\t\n209\tThis generic merge is safe for active generations only when both sides are still\n210\tadding checkpoints to the same generation. It is not used to publish a local\n211\trotation reset.\n212\t\n213\t## Remote Rotation Conflict Recovery\n214\t\n215\tA separate recovery path handles the case where another machine already rotated\n216\tand pushed the remote generation.\n217\t\n218\tThe shape looks like this:\n219\t\n220\t```text\n221\tStarting point:\n222\t  remote /full/current and local /full/current share C95\n223\t\n224\tMachine A:\n225\t  writes 7 more checkpoints\n226\t  rotates\n227\t  pushes /full/0000000000007 and fresh /full/current\n228\t\n229\tMachine B:\n230\t  still has old /full/current history\n231\t  writes 3 more checkpoints on top of C95\n232\t  pushes /full/current and gets non-fast-forward\n233\t```\n234\t\n235\tIf Machine B used the generic `/full/current` tree merge, the old generation\n236\twould be rehydrated into fresh remote `/full/current`, causing the same\n237\tcheckpoint IDs to appear in both an archived generation and the new current\n238\tgeneration.\n239\t\n240\tInstead, recovery detects remote archived generations and treats this as a\n241\tremote rotation conflict:\n242\t\n243\t1. List remote `/full/*` archive refs.\n244\t2. Treat missing local archives and same-name/different-hash archives as remote\n245\t   rotation evidence.\n246\t3. Fetch candidate archived generations with complete object graphs.\n247\t4. Find the archive that shares history with local `/full/current`.\n248\t5. Merge local `/full/current` into that archived generation.\n249\t6. Update `generation.json` timestamps in the merged archive.\n250\t7. Push the updated archive.\n251\t8. Adopt remote `/full/current` locally.\n252\t\n253\t### Example: User A Rotates, User B Has Local Work\n254\t\n255\t```text\n256\tShared base:\n257\t  /full/current has IDs 1..95\n258\t\n259\tUser A:\n260\t  adds IDs 96..102\n261\t  rotates and pushes\n262\t\n263\tRemote:\n264\t  /full/0000000000007 has IDs 1..102\n265\t  /full/current is a fresh orphan\n266\t\n267\tUser B:\n268\t  adds IDs 103..105 on top of the old current\n269\t  pushes and hits non-fast-forward"}]},"uuid":"975be16c-96f3-45a4-92fd-07e878ad65f6","timestamp":"2026-05-11T21:16:04.419Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/pfleidi/entire/cli/.worktrees/fix/checkpoints-v2-rotation-logic/docs/architecture/checkpoints-v2-rotation.md","content":"## Pre-Push Publication\n\nThe pre-push hook reads pending publications before pushing active v2 refs.\n\nFor pending local rotations, pre-push:\n\n1. Drops stale pending reset publications until the newest queued\n   `reset_full_current_root_hash` is in the live local `/full/current` history.\n   This covers the crash or failure window where a marker was recorded but the\n   reset never happened.\n2. Pushes the remaining pending archived generation refs together.\n3. Reads the current remote `/full/current` hash.\n4. Pushes local `/full/current` using `--force-with-lease`, guarded by the\n   expected old remote current hash.\n5. Removes the pending marker only after archive refs and the current reset are\n   published successfully.\n\nThe guarded current update is the key difference from a normal push. Replacing\nremote `/full/current` with a fresh orphan generation is not a fast-forward, so\nnormal `git push` rejects it. The lease makes the reset explicit and safe: it\nonly succeeds if the remote current is still the old generation covered by the\nlocal archive.\n\n### Example: Local Rotation With No New Work\n\n```text\nBefore rotation:\n  remote /full/current = C100\n  local  /full/current = C100\n\nLocal rotation:\n  local /full/0000000000007 = A7, parented on C100\n  local /full/current = N7, a fresh orphan root\n  pending marker expects remote current C100\n\nPre-push:\n  push /full/0000000000007\n  force-with-lease /full/current from C100 to N7\n  clear pending marker\n```\n\nFinal remote state:\n\n```text\